WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.050
SettlementService has two methods you want timed: settle and audit.

2
00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:11.140
Pasting a timer into each one, in every service, gets old fast.

3
00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:13.024
So you write a subclass.

4
00:00:13.024 --> 00:00:18.943
Its settle starts a clock, calls super.settle, and logs the time in a finally block.

5
00:00:18.943 --> 00:00:22.959
super.settle runs your original body, on the same object.

6
00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:28.699
Every method a subclass overrides is a place to run code before and after yours.

7
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.168
A framework does this without you writing the class.

8
00:00:32.168 --> 00:00:39.708
Byte Buddy builds the bytes of a new class at run time: a subclass of SettlementService that overrides settle and audit.

9
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:44.415
A class loader defines it, with a random suffix in its name on every run.

10
00:00:44.415 --> 00:00:49.141
The framework makes an instance, and your code just sees a SettlementService.

11
00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:51.783
Now a caller calls settle.

12
00:00:51.783 --> 00:00:52.983
What runs first?

13
00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:56.284
The generated override.

14
00:00:56.284 --> 00:01:02.289
It calls an interceptor, and hands it the method being called and a handle to the original.

15
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:06.033
The interceptor starts the clock, then calls the handle.

16
00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:11.900
That goes through an accessor method Byte Buddy added, into super.settle, and your body runs.

17
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:18.016
When your body returns, the interceptor logs the time and passes your result back to the caller.

18
00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:19.799
Now call region.

19
00:01:19.799 --> 00:01:22.756
It's final, so the subclass couldn't override it.

20
00:01:22.756 --> 00:01:26.256
The call goes straight to your body, and nothing times it.

21
00:01:26.933 --> 00:01:29.767
A final class can't be subclassed at all.

22
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:37.163
In Kotlin, classes and methods are final unless you write open, so Byte Buddy refuses a plain Kotlin class.

23
00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:41.033
The all-open compiler plugin opens the classes you mark.

24
00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:46.586
In code, you pick the class, the methods to override, and the interceptor.

25
00:01:46.586 --> 00:01:49.730
make writes the bytes, and load defines the class.

26
00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.553
Kotlin uses the same builder.

27
00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:57.694
The rule: a generated proxy is a subclass written at run time.

28
00:01:57.694 --> 00:02:04.104
Its overrides run code before and after yours, and what it can't override, it can't intercept.

29
00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.175
Quick check.

30
00:02:05.175 --> 00:02:09.039
A Kotlin class is open, but one of its methods isn't.

31
00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:10.571
Is that method timed?

32
00:02:12.767 --> 00:02:12.995
No.

33
00:02:12.995 --> 00:02:16.492
It's final, so the subclass can't override it.
